# Part 1: Creating a Fast Server

Many principles of a fast server, including configuring and securing Nginx, will remain the same.

## Rationale

Following these guidelines, developers and DevOps can enable users to interact with rendered content at a faster pace. This improves the user’s experience and can increase chances for user engagement with ads and other calls-to-action.

## Goals

- Understanding the Time to First Byte
- Implementing an efficient caching strategy with NGINX
- Balancing cache and security using NGINX and SSL

## Time to First Byte

When users click on a link or open up a URL, they expect to see content rapidly displayed. Time to First Byte (TTFB), is a metric commonly used for measuring a web server’s performance. It’s the time period from when a user submits a request to when a web server starts delivering the First Byte of a web page.

As shown above, time drags during TTFB. To improve TTFB and give users a faster perceived response time, optimize your web server to handle incoming requests efficiently. Follow NGINX best practices for boosting performance. Configure NGINX properly to load static content from disk cache without any CPU overhead. NGINX allows developers to configure multiple levels of cache, balancing a zen-like harmony between system performance and ease of operation for even the greenest of web developers.

## SSL

In the mobile-first world, the user experience becomes more crucial than ever. Endeavors to ensure a delightful user experience are less complicated when surrounding security and privacy issues. In fact, most people value security and privacy as a top concern. Adhering to user expectations of providing secure browsing requires developers to satisfy the growing de-facto standard of HTTPS enabled sites. The reduced performance found on HTTP/1+ and SSL stemming from the additional round-trip request to SSL certificate issuers has unfortunately sidetracked many developers from deploying HTTPS at a wider scale.

Moving forward, this will prove to be less likely as browsers begin requiring SSL (HTTP/2 aims to standardize on SSL). Rather than waiting until your hand is forced, refer to this performance comparison for deciding which SSL implementation to use for speed.

| SSL Implementation | Negotiation Time (HTTPS) | Negotiation Time (HTTP) | Benefits |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| OpenSSL | 2 more round trips | 0 | Native |
| NPN/TLS | 3 more round trips, Half-duplex | 0 | Nginx default |
| SPDY | 4 more round trips, Full-duplex | 0 | Chrome, Google Web Server variant |
| ALPN/TLS | 3 more round trips, Full-duplex | 0 | Set by default on nginx-alpn |
| NPN-Fallback/TLS | 3 more round trips, Full-duplex | 0 | Discontinued |

Figure 1.2 - HTTP vs HTTPS implementation on NGINX

Enhance server response times using NPN/TLS. With NPN/TLS, after the client sends the ClientHello, and the web server SSL handshake effectively delivers a record in response, handling the incoming request improves faster. NPN/TLS improves download speeds at least comparable to HTTP/1.x and Helps web developers better balance caching and security with HTTPS.
[46/200] Running: Explain transformer decoder blocks layer-by-layer....

🧠 LLM Response:


A Transformer Decoder block in the Transformer model is responsible for generating the target sequence given a source sequence. It consists of multiple sub-layers, each with its unique functionality. The decoder block includes the following layers:

1. Multi-head self-attention (MHA): This layer helps the decoder to focus on different positions in the target sequence and capture dependencies between them. The self-attention mechanism allows the model to weigh the importance of each word in the target sequence concerning other words in the same sequence. The MHA layer computes the attention scores in parallel for all attention heads and then combines them using a linear layer. The output of the MHA layer is then passed through a residual connection, layer normalization, and a feed-forward network.
2. Encoder-Decoder attention (EDA): To capture the dependencies between the source and target sequences, the decoder includes an encoder-decoder attention layer that uses the encoder output as the source sequence and the previous decoder layer output as the target sequence. The EDA layer computes the attention scores for each word in the target sequence concerning all words in the source sequence and then combines the scores using a linear layer. Similar to the MHA layer, the output of the EDA layer is passed through a residual connection, layer normalization, and a feed-forward network.
3. Feed-forward network (FFN): The FFN layer applies a non-linear transformation to the output of the previous layer. The FFN consists of two linear layers separated by a ReLU activation function. This layer allows the model to capture non-linear dependencies in the data and learn more complex representations of the sequence.

These three layers are stacked together to create a single decoder block, and multiple decoder blocks can be stacked together to create a deeper model. It's worth noting that the Transformer decoder has a causal constraint, which means that the output at time step t can only depend on inputs up to time step t. This constraint is enforced by masking the attention scores in the MHA and EDA layers so that each position in the target sequence can only attend to previous positions.
[47/200] Running: What is a KV cache in transformer inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:


During transformer inference (as part of NLP, packing mandatory), all the pos-embed vectors are cached.
This allows O(1) look up because these vectors are arranged as keys in a KV cache mapping.

Could someone explain more about the KV cache:

- How is this achieved?
- What does it help to do?
- Is it prioritizing certain parts to look at from data?
- Etc.

Original question is here: https://discuss.huggingface.co/t/transformer-inference-standfor-kvcache-what-does-it-mean/8099

Answer:

KV cache in the context of the Hugging Face Transformers library is used in the tokenizer in the function `encode_plus` and it stands for "Key, Value cache". It stores in a dictionary the embeddings for all the tokens in the vocabulary. This allows for very efficient creation of the input sequence of token embeddings, since it does not have to recompute all the embeddings for each forward pass. This data structure is designed to speedup inference by storing embeddings so that they are readily available.

Here are the details:

- How is this achieved?: The `encode_plus` method of the transformer tokenizer (BertTokenizerFast or any of its variants) creates the input\_ids and attention\_mask tensor for the given input and then extracts the embeddings for all the tokens in the given input in the form of activations using `self.embeddings.forward(input_ids)`. These embeddings are stored in the `self.key_values` tensor as a dictionary with the token ids as keys. This is done only once when invoking this funciton for the first time. On subsequent calls, instead of forwarding through the `embeddings` block, this tensor is reused.
- What does it help to do?: It speeds up the inference by not requiring to recompute the embeddings for each forward pass.
- Is it prioritizing certain parts to look at from data?: No, it is not prioritizing certain parts to look at from data. Once the embeddings have been calculated once (on the first call of `encode_plus` method for a new instance of tokenizer), they will be readily available as-is.

The summary is that the KV cache is a feature added, so that transformer model can speed up inference for your use case by storing embeddings of tokens that are used frequently, so that they do not have to be recomputed.
[48/200] Running: Describe token-by-token generation and its challenges....

🧠 LLM Response:

What are the challange associated with application generating text using sequence to sequence models?
Token by token generation is a framework for natural language generation that consists in predicting a sequence letter by letter or word by word. Since machine learning models don’t literally write the text letter or word by letter, token-by-token generation is implemented as a supervised learning classification problem, with each token (letter or word) as a model input and a distribution over possible token outputs.
Token-by-token generation has the following challenges:
Handling of “unknown unknowns”. Models cannot predict tokens/words that were never observed during training.
Handling of verification, i.e. checking if text makes sense while generated characters/words are added. This is typically more challenging with character-based models.
Exposure bias during training: models will only have seen the true next word in the sequence during training and may struggle to produce coherent text when away from the training set.
Exposure bias during generating text and trivializing the training process due to feedback loops. During training, models are likely to produce more accurate text than during evaluation because newer, more accurate inputs are generated. This can lead to misunderstandings in how well the model actually performs, as during evaluation there is no such improvement during the predictive generation process.
Hard to optimize between prediction and readability. Text predicted is often done so from the perspective of predicting next characters, rather than optimizing readability and coherence of the predicted text.
Applying attention and other stateful architectures. Models that apply context from previous steps of sequence can be hard to implement.
Text normalization. Predictive generative models may struggle with different variations of same word. For example, abbreviations (e.g. "st." vs. "street"), camel case (e.g. "EmailResponse" vs. "email response"), and case variants (e.g. "John Doe" vs. "john doe"). Handling these variations is necessary for predictive models to "understand" text in the same way humans do.
Generating an application from sequence to sequence model can face several challenges related to token-by-token generation:
1. Exposure bias: during training, models predict the token (letter or word) following a sequence but it only has the true token as input. During generating words, models generate the new token and use this new token as input to generate the next token. Since there's a gap between both scenarios, the generated text quality can deteriorate quickly.
2. Not seeing the whole text: as models generate words or character by character, it doesn't have access to the whole generated text. It makes it hard to apply attention mechanisms or other techniques that require a full text context.
3. Quality metrics: understanding quality of generated text includes readability, coherence, or other subjective metrics, which makes evaluation challenging.
4. Lack of diversity: models can generate similar text because they don't have variability in the input or because they've been trained in similar text.
5. Lack of common sense or knowledge: as models learn from text, they rarely have a global knowledge of the world or common sense reasoning. This makes models generate sometimes invalid text.
6. Human interference: some models may still need human labels or a human in the loop, to ensure appropriate output and prevent biased behavior.
