Principal Components of Transformers
A transformer model is based on the idea of self-attention. This method lets the model analyze every input in relation to the others, unlike recurrent neural networks, which process them sequentially. Each transformer block contains a self-attention mechanism and a feed-forward network. More precisely:
1. Word embeddings: the text input is transformed into what is called word embeddings. You can think of each word as a vector that encodes both semantic and syntactic information.
2. Encoder-Decoder: After obtaining embeddings, they pass through an encoder-decoder setup. Encoders and decoders are just stacked transformer blocks.
3. Transformer Block: for each block, first feature-rich context is computed using the embedding inputs via self-attention. This allows for interactions between all the words in the input. The second part consists of a one-layer feed-forward network allowing for more flexibility and control to the model, which then outputs a new word encoding.
4. Output representation: the final output of the word encodings are then transformed into a probability distribution across a complete vocabulary of words.
Multilingual NLP with Transformers
