Some documents call this attention mechanism "Efficient Attention", for example, the `flash_attention` package. However, since most of the other papers and implementations call it "flash attention", I will use that name throughout this essay.

tl;dr: Flash attention has a constant time complexity ($O(1)$) for computing attention, which is achieved by automatically dividing the input into non-overlapping parts called flashes. Because of this, flash attention can be more interpretable and faster than self-attention in many use cases. Despite this, flash attention makes some assumptions to reach this constant time complexity, so it isn't a clear winner over self-attention, but it is an interesting variation.

## Self-Attention Mechanisms

The original Transformer architecture from Vaswani et al. (2017) used multi-headed self-attention, as follows: (1) first, for each input, we denote it a query $q$, a key $k$, and a value $v$: (2) dot product $q \cdot k^\intercal$ is performed, and then divide it by the sqrt of the key dimension: $\frac{q \cdot k^\intercal}{\sqrt{d_k}}$, (3) applying a softmax activation, and (4) finally, we can get the weighted sum of $v$, which is the final result.

To generalize this process: the query, key, and value are processed through three different linear layers before we execute the matrix multiplication or the weighted sum steps. Furthermore, we don't have to limit ourselves to seeing input only with three scenes. Instead, we can detect multiple key sequences using different linear layers (called heads) to extract different types of hidden relationships. The last dimension of the output, which we will call the Linear Layer I, will be viewed as a combination of all heads, as shown below.

Because the self-attention mechanism needs all input at the same time, it has a time complexity of $O(n^2)$ (where $n$ is the input length). This means that the time required grows quadratically with the length of the input. When you cross around a thousand tokens in practice, you can expect self-attention to break down.

There are two ways to make the self-attention mechanism more scalable while maintaining its quadratic time complexity.

1. restricting the maximum number of sequence positions that will interact during the self-attention process, which is implemented by taking chunks of the input. For example, the Longformer model restricts itself to 4096.
2. reducing the number of multiplications that need to result in the dot product heads * $fwd$.

There is a recent implementation (Broad and Heafield, 2022) that removes this restriction in the self-attention process. This is done through Local Sensitivity Hashing (Driebeek and Ftaoussi, 2018), reducing $O(n^2)$ to approximately $O(n \log n)$.

However, neither of these two methods substantially decreases the quadratic dependency on input length. Even with caching mechanisms such as Longformer or those in the prior work (Ram et al. (2021) and Wood et al. (2021)), there is a considerable overhead to send all the data, even when it is repeated.

Efforts have been made to maintain the $O(n)$ linear time complexity with caching mechanisms while improving their time and space complexities (Tay et al. (2020) and Beltagy et al. (2020)). However, this overhead is reduced by flash attention in a more drastic way by dividing the input into large non-overlapping chunks called flashes.

## Flash Attention Mechanisms

Ding et al. (2022) recently introduced a partitioning strategy-based approach to divide the input into disjoint segments called flashes. The flash attention mechanism uses a discretization of the input to calculate an attention-like score. This maintains attention's desired properties but alleviates the burden of dealing with an entire input. Flash cannot work well if discretized with too coarse or too fine a granularity. Flash uses a discretization strategy to balance the trade-off and get the
