🧠 LLM Response:


Inference throughput is the number of images or videos that is processed by an inference solution such as TensorRT™ in one second.  It is often used as a measure to compare the performance of different inference solutions.

The inference throughput is either calculated on an inference-only development setup (recommended) or on deployed systems, for which measuring tools and methodologies need to be adopted.  On a development setup, TensorRT provides built-in APIs and sample code to measure inference throughput, and each DL framework typically provides the recommended way of measuring inference throughput.

The inference throughput depends on many factors such as the specific inference model, the size of the input/output data, batch size, number of available CUDA cores, compute capabilities of the GPU, TF32 or FP16 datatype, and clock speed.

For TensorRT inference engines that use a single sample Input network (UFF, ONNX, TensorFlow), inference throughput can be measured as follows:

1. Measure the latency (time taken in microseconds from input generation to output availability) when feeding a single sample to the inference engine. This value is the reciprocal of batch size.

2. Determine the overhead or extra latency caused by starting or resetting the inference engine. This overhead can be measured by dividing the latency of inference with a single sample by the overall process waiting time and multiplying the reciprocal by the measurement overhead in microseconds (can be measured at the end of each inference cycle).

3. Calculate the inference throughput as reciprocal of the latency timestamp differentials inclusive of the estimated overhead.  Subtract the estimated start time of the first inference from the estimated completion time of each inference to get each latency timestamp differential. Average the first latency differential because the associated CPU tasks with start overhead is usually more than normal between inferences.

4. Use CUDA profiling tools such as CUDA profiler nvprof, CUDA-GDB, or nvtx_range_api to identify and improve any overhead-inducing bottlenecks such as transfer times from CPU to GPU memory, or backpressure from excessive triggering of events and semaphores. Compute the effective persistent inference-only throughput to discriminate latency-inducing bottlenecks that would otherwise be hidden in the estimated overhead.

5. This compute the inference throughput as reciprocal of the latency timestamp differentials inclusive of the estimated overhead based on the dataset. Average latency timestamp differentials must reconcile with the number of processed elements if using datasets. The difference in periodic samples divided by the average latency times differentials will yield inference throughput for 3D volume datasets.

Inference throughput for reference TensorRT sample applications can be found in the sections for each TensorRT sample on NVIDIA Developer Zone

NOTE:

1. In TensorRT 6, the ENGINELAYERBEGIN event was depreciated, and timing using ENGINELAYERBEGIN should now use ENGINELAYEREND. This change does not affect the throughput calculation.

2. The thermal limit would affect the peak performance on some GPUs. This is evaluated by monitoring the temperature rise of the GPU while stress testing inference tasks in order to maintain safe thermal limits.

Algorithmic Complexity:

Complexity of state and reduction networks:Several networks have a very small processing volume compared to the network’s computational volume, so calculating throughput from latency is not suitable.

