[67/200] Running: Explain the impact of batch size on training and inference....

🧠 LLM Response:


Batch size refers to the number of samples used in one iteration of training a model. The choice of batch size has implications for both the training and inference phases of a machine learning model.

Training:

* Smaller batch sizes (e.g. 1-32) generally result in slower convergence during training, but can lead to better generalization and a more robust model. This is because using smaller batch sizes introduces more randomness into the training process, which can help the model escape local minima and find a better global minimum.
* Larger batch sizes (e.g. 256-4096) generally result in faster convergence during training, but can lead to worse generalization and a less robust model. This is because using larger batch sizes reduces the amount of randomness in the training process, which can cause the model to get stuck in local minima and not find the best global minimum.
* Using a batch size that is too large can also result in numerical instability during training, as the gradients become too large and can cause the model's weights to explode or vanish.

Inference:

* During inference, the batch size is typically set to 1, as each sample is processed independently. However, in some cases, using a larger batch size during inference can improve the throughput of the model, as multiple samples can be processed simultaneously.
* Using a larger batch size during inference can also introduce more randomness into the inference process, which can help to improve the model's robustness to noisy input data.
* However, using a larger batch size during inference can also increase the latency of the model, as more samples need to be processed before a prediction can be made.

In summary, the choice of batch size has a significant impact on the training and inference phases of a machine learning model. Smaller batch sizes can lead to better generalization and a more robust model during training, but can result in slower convergence and increased latency during inference. Larger batch sizes can lead to faster convergence and increased throughput during training and inference, but can result in worse generalization and a less robust model. Therefore, it is important to carefully consider the trade-offs when choosing a batch size for a machine learning model.
[68/200] Running: What are fused kernels and why are they fast?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Fused kernels are a compute primitive in which multiple loops or computational operations are combined (fused) into a single kernel which executes on a GPU (or other accelerator).    Previously, these tasks were “un-fused” and performed as separate operations.   This meant multiple memory transfers and synchronization between each compute operation.   Fused kernels have the benefit of being “memory friendly” in that multiple un-fused operations result in more memory transfers compared to just a single, memory transfer for a fused kernel.   All the combined operations share the same input and output data (memory), and hence, only needs to be moved once.  Additionally, the memory hierarchy within a GPU significantly drives the performance of GPU (see https://developer.nvidia.com/deep-learning-performance).   The memory hierarchy of a GPU includes global (device), shared, constant, cache, and register memory.   The global memory space has typical read/write bandwidth is much lower than shared memory bandwidth (~4x to 10x).   Since multiple un-fused operations generate multiple global memory accesses, cumulative memory bandwidth saturation occurs and prevents jobs from growing any larger.

Many GPU deep learning libraries have been “fused”.   From our observations of training large neural networks (GNNs), building and training with fused kernels result in a speed up of ~2x over the same architecture with un-fused kernels.

ROCm (GPU) Example

AMD’s ROCM (see https://rocmdocs.amd.com/en/latest/Programming%20Guides/CL/clPlusPlus.html) is a good example of a fused primative.   ROCM provides the “hipReduce” function which performs a reduction operation on data.  This function is commonly used when you sum (accumulate) arrays and vectors.  Under the hood, hipReduce fuses multiple compute operations (reduce, broadcast, and scatter) into a single kernel.   Each un-fused operation has memory transactions (broadcast and scatter).  Using this fused operation results in reduced memory transactions, resulting in a faster execution.

MV Apollon gRPC Native Example

MV Apollon provides an API extension to Tensorflow (Open-source) and PyTorch (Open-source) called graph-convolution-ast.  This extension enables communication between the multinodes using gRPC Native messages-transport over Mellanox InfiniBand.

Many operations in gRPC Native (Send and receive messages) were previously un-fused.  Each Type-Length-Value (TLV) message was extracted from the full message, resulting in un-optimized transmissions.   Now, the gRPC Native graphics API was updated with optimizations, fusing the extraction, aggregation, and send-receive operations into a single message component.

We have observed these optimizations and fused kernels result in a combined 2x overall performance improvement.

1. This benchmark is based on the NVIDIA A100 GPU.
2. This result also includes the free GD-Batcher which is available https://www.mellanox.com/software-for-ml/-/media/Software%20For%20ML/Software%20For%20ML/Images/GD-Batcher-Intro-2021.pdf as a software library or in ROCM firmware version 5.2.2 an later.

Benchmark: MultiNode Communication with ZFP

One use case of fused kernels involves ZFP floating point compression.   ZFP is a GPU accelerated deep floating-point sparse-compression library developed by Lawrence Livermore National Laboratories.   ZFP can compress a tensor (4D array) by more than 500x for the largest tensors.   However, compressing and then decompressing a tensor in GPU memory still takes a significant amount of time and would increase graph reduction communication time.   Decompression is always done on the target machine.

Much of the communication time is spent on the actual graph-reduce communication operations.  These are functions created by Tensorflow and PyTorch where communication between nodes occur.  MV Apollon gRPC Native extension implements graph-convolution-ast and includes new fused kernels for ZFP which optimizes ZFP transmissions.

