[70/200] Running: What are tensor parallel and tensor slicing?...

🧠 LLM Response:


When developing models for distributed training, tensor parallel and tensor slicing are techniques that can help solve limitations with the way sequential models scale, as they try to better use the computational power of available hardware.

In the following stages, we will go over two specific distributed training techniques that involve tensor slicing.

Configuration:
Tensor Parallelism

We start by discussing tensor parallelism, a form of parallelism applied at the very start of model training.

When developing models for sequential training, a large tensor is produced by the forward model.

When several sequential models are combined in data parallelism, the largest tensor is computed for each batch of data, and this tensor is transmitted over the network to each participating GPU to perform typical backprop calculations.

This process can be quite expensive in terms of computational resources since the largest tensor is replicated on each GPU.

Tensor parallelism is a technique that distributes parts of the forward model over several GPUs to allow the largest tensor created by the model to be smaller and more manageable. This is accomplished by distributing and processing the large tensor across several GPUs so that we can obtain the largest tensor required by the model.

This is done using a special tensor parallel wrapper which divides the model into submodels allowing both forward and backward passes to work across multiple GPUs.

Configuration:
Pipeline Parallelism

However, when working with incredibly big models or constrained by the number of available GPUs, tensor parallelism may not be sufficient. Tensor slicing and pipeline parallelism, a second form of parallelism that we can use in conjunction with tensor parallelism, can be an alternative.

In pipeline parallelism, the sequential model is divided into smaller models that are placed on different GPUs. A popular method to divide these models is by the layer count of each sequential model.

These smaller models will still run in sequence, waiting for the signal to pass from one model to the next. At each pass, a barrier is set so that the next model does not run until all previous models have completed.

Tension parallel and pipeline parallel can be applied at different scales. If we need just a few more GPUs to train the model using tensor parallelism, an additional GPU can be easily added to speed up the process. However, pipeline parallelism requires a more dramatic setup and usually uses more GPUs.

In summary, tensors can be easily sliced for parallelism using either the traditional tensor slicing methods or the pipeline parallelism method. These techniques can be crucial for handling large models using distributed training.

Overall, our TensorFlow and PyTorch tutorials cover how to apply these techniques.
