[72/200] Running: What are inference graphs in deep learning?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Deep learning models are usually comprised of multiple layers where each layer processes a certain aspect of the incoming data. To make predictions on new data, most deep learning models require a forward pass, starting from the very first layer on the data, through all the other layers, and finally to the last layer, which outputs the hypothesis (also known as a prediction).

The idea behind inference graphs is that the idea of a forward pass, as it typically exists today, is inefficient. Instead, inference graphs tries take advantage of the semantic meaning in the layers of a deep learning model, and how they interact, to extract, simplify, and rearrange the information they process. In the manner that x^{2} - 3x - 4 can be rearranged to (x - 4)(x + 1), and that x_{t + 1} = (1 - r) x_t + n can be iterated, inference graphs aim to transform deep learning models from a sequential data processing pipeline to a more flexible representation with the ability to be edited, extended, or reorganized at will.

Inference graphs accomplish this by breaking down each layer into small patterns of data processing and representing them using highly-optimized structures. From there, the layers are connected to other layers' computations using a similar method. Most graph-based systems we know and love in machine learning (and in computer science in general for that matter) come in the form of neural networks and Bayesian networks, but inference graphs are neither, but rather its own invention.

 Deep learning models are awesome, but have major inefficiencies in the way they are formulated and presented in code. For instance, in science, the burden is on the scientist to keep hypothesis testing simple to their implementation, or identically, on the programmer to make data processing pipelines simple to maintain. But when we want to build large models with many layers that interact in complicated ways, algorithmic conventions don't afford us the luxury to do this all too easily. As a result, we've often resorted to training parameters how we want to (e.g. using He et al. initialization over normal initialization), and often hack together solutions for getting data to the model in forms that it likes.

The dream, of course, is when we want to make changes to the initial layer, we can just edit a single layer during inference, without tweaking the hyperparameters for layers that come after. Deep inference is the solution to make all this happen.

By doing this, we can have layers be entirely independent of their upstream layers (similar to the how the graph structure of a random forest isn't affected by the input features, and the structure of decision trees aren't influenced by the levels below them), we can have our own custom layers (inspired by Tensorflow's custom layers), and we can incorporate standard data processing operations like horizontal flips, random crops, color shifts, and photometric distortions. Part of the reason that a model like ResNet-50 would take so long would be that we have to re-run the entire sequential pipeline even if we want to add a simple layer. But if - for example - we want it to be a mirror filtered image classification model, all we have to do is add the mirror flip layer right during inference time.

We can also give layers a deeper semantic meaning by allowing operations that only make sense in the context in which they were specified. For instance, we can add small adjustments to a color shift layer without adding the same adjustments to every layer, or we can specify a particular layer to be used with a specific subset of data (e.g. using the "cat" dataset pre-trained model on the "dog" dataset, but only recalibrating the "cat" model without using the "dog" data to retrain all the layers that came before).

The idea that this could make GPU training faster is unreasonable, and goes against the core principles of deep learning model training. That is, it would essential boil down to "why would this technique be any faster than running everything from the start?" The answer is because inference graphs are a novel hybrid of deep learning and graph convolutions. Instead of just running the same operations that the input mandatory requires, we can define operations that can be in a state of optimization.

By excavating the innards of a deep learning model using rigorous, mathematically-based graph theory, then, we can make deep learning much larger, much more efficient, and most significantly, much more flexible.

