[80/200] Running: How is multilingual performance evaluated in LLMs?...

🧠 LLM Response:


There exist several challenge sets or multilingual tasks geared towards LLMs, such as XTREME. These tasks contain non-English (e.g. Arabic, Chinese) datasets and therefore computed evaluation metrics typically include non-English languages. Evaluation metrics for MLMs that only have english datasets (e.g. BERT) will not take into account multilingualism.

For example, in SuperGLUE, there is the B sentence completion task (RTE). Given sentences A and B, if A entails B, respond with yes, otherwise no. The English version of the RTE dataset will only compute scores for English, but not for Chinese.

To motivate why multilingual performance is important, consider the Google Translate sentiment analysis model. In this work, a word-embedding model is trained and the final model incorporates a weighted combination of multiple source language models. The sentiment analysis output for a given sentence in a source language is such that a sentence with negative sentiment has a lower score than one with a positive sentiment. Google Translate leverages this word-embedding to provide a sentiment analysis service, but in doing so, realizes that some languages exhibit the opposite behavior (for example, positive-sentiment words in Japanese have a higher word embedding space score than negative-sentiment words).

Therefore, it is impractical to assess multilingual performance through using a single language, since one language might have drastically different properties to another.

However, could it be the case that the multilingual performance of an MLM might be concentrated over a particular subset of languages?

The answer is no, unless the subset of languages is extremely narrow. The only trained MLMs that exhibit such behavior train only a subset of languages due to hardware and memory constraints. This MLM is named mBERT-lite.

To summarize, multilingual performance evaluation by definition assesses the performance of LLMs over any supported language. It is important due to the inherent multilingual feature of LLMs and can never be approximated by a single-language performance evaluation. Multilingual performance is explainable, diverse and diffuse over all languages.
[81/200] Running: Describe an example use of prompt tuning....

🧠 LLM Response:


Prompt tuning is a method for adapting pre-trained language models to specific NLP tasks without modifying the model architecture or fine-tuning the entire model. Prompt tuning involves constructing a task-specific input prompt and learning a set of continuous task-specific vectors, called prompt vectors, that are added to the embedding of the input prompt.

For example, consider the task of sentiment analysis. In prompt tuning, you might construct an input prompt that looks like this: "The movie was [MASK]". The [MASK] token is a special token that the pre-trained language model will learn to fill in with a word or phrase that completes the sentence.

Next, you would initialize a set of prompt vectors, one for each possible sentiment (e.g. positive, negative, neutral). These prompt vectors are added to the embedding of the [MASK] token, and the resulting sequence is input to the pre-trained language model. The language model will generate a probability distribution over possible completions for the [MASK] token, and you can use the maximum likelihood completion as the predicted sentiment.

During training, the prompt vectors are learned using gradient descent. The goal is to adjust the prompt vectors so that the pre-trained language model is more likely to generate completions that are indicative of the correct sentiment.

Prompt tuning has been shown to be an effective way to adapt pre-trained language models to a wide range of NLP tasks, including sentiment analysis, question answering, and named entity recognition. It is a lightweight alternative to fine-tuning the entire model, which can be time-consuming and computationally expensive.
[82/200] Running: What is the attention bottleneck and how to address it?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Deep neural networks are adept at learning powerful feature representations for a variety of tasks from images, texts, videos, and audios. The attention mechanism has been extensively used to improve the performance of these neural networks when dealing with sequential data such as natural language processing and speech recognition tasks. The attention mechanism selects the most relevant information in the input sequence, allowing the network to focus on the most contextually relevant parts of the input data given a task at hand. Intuitively, the attention mechanism allows the system to make (noisy) local predictions at a reasonable cost while avoiding the need to perform global predictions.

The attentional feature representations produced by these neural networks are critical to the end task performance. For example, in language translation, the translation is determined by a sequence of words from the source language. The attention mechanism learns which of those words are most relevant to the meaning of the translated target sequence. In video understanding, the prediction is determined a sequence of video frames. The attention mechanism learns which of those frames are most relevant to the prediction at hand (say, high-level action classification, object detection or segmentation, etc.). It has been shown that high-quality and discriminative feature representations are extremely beneficial for end tasks such as video prediction, captioning and action classification. Feature representations are often learned through self-supervised tasks such as language modeling, reconstruction, auto-encoding and masked image modeling. In this blog post, we will take a deep dive into `Efficiently Attentive Transformers (EATs)`, a paper proposing strategies to effectively overcome the attention bottleneck.

Note: The term `attention bottleneck` refers to the attention mechanism learning (global) feature representations that are bottlenecked due to the inadequate capacity of the (local) feature representations.

- Paper: Efficiently Attentive Transformers (EATs) [Link]
- Code: Linked in the above repo

# 1. Introduction

The authors propose that an effective strategy to overcome the attention bottleneck is to ensure (global) features are consistently disentangled on major factors of human perception in the input sequences, such as objects or speech scenes. Disentangled feature representations automatically decompose into perceptually meaningful factors, thereby making them naturally more discriminative. In contrast, bottleneck features are less discriminative due to egocentricity, which refers to attention depending on the learned features rather than on perceptual factors.

The authors begin by motivating their work through the illustration of vanilla self-attention in Figure 1 and claiming it contains several flaws. They specifically point out the tendency of the vanilla mechanism learning to focus on (a) the same speakers (for audio) or the same characters (for video), (b) the same objects or characters, or (c) nearby (in space or time) frame regions. With respect to the figure below, captions (b), (c), and (d), are flawed as they are repetitively attending to the same object/action, whereas the correct caption (a) has an appropriate cycle of attention.

The authors propose a simple self-attention mechanism to enable,

1. effective learning of discriminative global feature representations,
2. ensure each self-attention function attends to (maximum) one object/scene (in time or space), and
3. consistently focus on spatially or temporally isolated objects/scenes.

The proposed Efficiently Attentive Transformers (EATs) obtain superior performance when evaluated on various representation learning tasks on YouTube-BoundingBoxes, raw YouTube videos, and Audioset datasets. The authors use a combination of distance correlation and normalized random walks to elegantly address the aforementioned limitations of the vanilla self-attention mechanism.

# 2. Key Innovations

This section describes the proposed innovations in the paper,

### 2.1. Relative Distance Correlation

The paper uses a relative distance metric to regularize the vanilla self-attention mechanism on image/video size and audio length and biases it to select subsequent languages or frames. In the following figure, the quantiles are used to sort all the distances between frames in a video with length T. \(\alpha\_1, \alpha\_2, \alpha\_3, .., \alpha\_k\) are used to refer to the \(\frac{1}{k}\) quantiles of all the frame distances.

