[87/200] Running: What are stop sequences in generation APIs?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Stop sequences are a way to control the length of the response. 

When making a request to a generation API, you can specify a list of stop sequences that the API should use to determine when to stop generating text. Once the API generates a sequence of characters that matches one of the stop sequences, it will stop generating text and return the response.

Stop sequences are useful for controlling the length of the generated text, especially in cases where you want to generate a specific number of words or sentences. For example, you might use the stop sequence "\n\n" to indicate that the API should stop generating text after it generates two newline characters, which would correspond to the end of a paragraph.

Here's an example of how you might use stop sequences in a request to a generation API:
```bash
POST /api/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "prompt": "Once upon a time",
  "stop_sequences": ["\n\n", "the end"]
}
```
In this example, the API will stop generating text when it encounters either "\n\n" (two newline characters, indicating the end of a paragraph) or "the end" (a specific phrase that indicates the end of the story).

Note that the use of stop sequences is optional, and you can choose to omit them if you don't need to control the length of the generated text. However, if you do use stop sequences, be sure to choose them carefully to ensure that they don't accidentally trigger the API to stop generating text prematurely.
[88/200] Running: Describe token streaming latency optimization....

🧠 LLM Response:


* Create the following topics:

        ```
        users
        userInfo
        userToken
        ```

* Create subscriptions to the following topics (all requests must be made for a single connection):

        ```
        PRODUCER_CONNECTION.subscribe(users + "->" + userToken)
        PRODUCER_CONNECTION.subscribe(users + "->" + userInfo)
        CONSUMER_CONNECTION.subscribe(userToken + "->" + users)
        CONSUMER_CONNECTION.subscribe(userInfo + "->" + users)
        ```

* Use [BatchProcessor](https://literatejava.com/apache-mq/using-batchprocessor-with-kafka-connect) to optimize the processing of obtained Tokens for efficient stream message processing (let messages live in the queue until the full payload is obtained, then process all message batches as a single normalized payload). The key to the optimization is to ensure exactly once semantics for the batch. Use the [BatchValueTransformer](https://github.com/max-grinev/magicka/blob/master/kafka-connect/src/main/java/org/kafka/connect/transform/BatchValueTransformer.java) to normalize and the [ExactlyOnceTransformer](https://github.com/max-grinev/magicka/blob/master/kafka-connect/src/main/java/org/kafka/connect/transform/StreamsTransformer.java) to ensure batch exactly once semantics.

* Separate token producer from token consumer by using a dedicated consumer application (using the KafkaConsumer component).

* Use Json instead of Avro for schema evolution serialization in Kafka Connect - required to handle late arriving schemas.

* Use SMT (stream processing transformations) to add a dynamic schema to late arriving token data records, using the schema to manage  Kafka record headers ser/deser (since dynamic schemas are often late arriving, and the schema must define the record payload: field headers)
