[108/200] Running: What are residual connections in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Residual connections, also known as shortcut connections, are a type of connection used in neural networks that allow the output of one layer to be added to the input of the next layer. This helps to alleviate the problem of vanishing gradients, which can occur when training deep neural networks.

In transformers, residual connections are used in the self-attention and feed-forward layers. The self-attention layer takes in a sequence of input vectors and generates a set of output vectors, where each output vector is a weighted sum of the input vectors. The feed-forward layer, on the other hand, applies a linear transformation to the input vectors, followed by a non-linear activation function.

By adding the output of one layer to the input of the next layer, residual connections allow the gradient to be directly backpropagated through the layers, rather than having to pass through multiple layers of non-linear transformations. This helps to ensure that the gradient is not vanishing or exploding, which can make it difficult to train deep neural networks.

Residual connections are an important component of transformers, and have been shown to improve the performance of these models on a variety of natural language processing tasks.

In summary, residual connections in transformers are a type of connection that allows the output of one layer to be added to the input of the next layer. This helps to alleviate the problem of vanishing gradients, and has been shown to improve the performance of transformers on natural language processing tasks.
