To examine latency in model output, you can measure the time interval between the input provided to the model and the model's output response. This can be observed by running the model multiple times with the same input and checking the consistent differences in the time taken for the model to generate the output.

Here's an example using Python and a hypothetical machine learning model `my_model`:

```python
import time

input_data = ...  # Your input data here
n_iterations = 10

total_latency = 0

for i in range(n_iterations):
    start_time = time.time()
    my_model.predict(input_data)
    end_time = time.time()

    latency = end_time - start_time
    total_latency += latency

average_latency = total_latency / n_iterations

print(f"Average latency: {average_latency:.5f} seconds")
```

Replace `my_model` with your actual machine learning model and `input_data` with your actual input data. This example will run your model 10 times and calculate the average latency.

Note: The time taken might be affected by various external factors like system load, other running processes, etc. To minimize the impact of these factors, consider running the code in a controlled environment with less background load.

If you are making real-time predictions using a skilled web service or similar infrastructure, you can also measure latency using HTTP requests. In such cases, you can measure time using the `time` library in your chosen programming language or utilities such as `curl`:

```bash
curl -o /dev/null -s -w "Time taken: %{time_total}s\n" -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"input_data": ...}' http://your-model-endpoint
```

Replace `http://your-model-endpoint` with the actual endpoint URL and `...` with your actual input data as a JSON string. This example will return the time taken to complete the HTTP request, effectively measuring latency.
