## Answer (-1)

Log probability (short for logarithmic probability) is the logarithm of probability. It's often used in cases where probabilities are very small, e.g. in language modelling.

The reason why log probabilities are used instead of normal probabilities is that it allows a more precise measurement to be recorded. For example, if the probability of a given outcome is 0.0000000000001, it would be difficult to represent such a small number with lots of statistical significance. The logarithm of such a number gives us a much more manageable figure (in this case of magnitude -20), and it's easy to represent it with high statistical significance.

Another reason for using log probabilities is that they can be manipulated mathematically much more easily, e.g. when calculating the joint probability of two events. The logarithm converts multiplication operations into simple addition.
