[136/200] Running: How does `max_new_tokens` differ from `max_length`?...

🧠 LLM Response:


When specifying a model using the OpenAI Python library, you can control its output characteristics in various ways. Two such parameters are `max_new_tokens` and `max_length`.

`max_new_tokens` is a limit on the number of tokens that the model can generate in a single API call. This parameter directly affects how much new content the model will produce beyond your prompt, since the prompt itself also counts towards this total.

`max_length`, on the other hand, controls the maximum length of the total generation, including your prompt. If the generated tokens, when combined with your prompt, exceed this limit, an error will be returned and no text will be produced. The difference is that the actual number of generated tokens can be less than `max_tokens` to stay under the `max_length` limit, while with `max_new_tokens`, the model will always generate as close to that limit as possible.

In summary, `max_new_tokens` sets a strict limit on how much new text the model can generate, while `max_length` sets an upper bound on the entire sequence length.
[137/200] Running: Explain the difference between loss and perplexity....

🧠 LLM Response:
 Loss is a scalar value calculated directly from the observed data and the model, capturing in a single quantity how well the model explains the data. Low loss means that the model explains the data well and high loss that it doesn’t. As an example, loss for linear regression is calculated as the average squared error between the predicted value and the real value. This makes it so that for a single wrong prediction the loss can be a large value, although the overall loss can still be low if the model normally makes correct predictions.

Perplexity however is a normalization of the loss to make it more comparable between models. Just like loss, lower perplexity means that the model is better. Since the loss is an average, the interpretation of the perplexity depends on how the loss is calculated. In the case of linear regression, lower perplexity means that the predictions are more certain.

Since perplexity is meant to be an easier to read form of the loss, it is often used as the measurement of performance in competitions in which the model that performs best wants to be identified. Those competitions will have clear, well defined conditions and metrics, so that the perplexity can be calculated the same way for all models.

Perplexity is often defined as 2 to the power of the cross-entropy error. This is equivalent to exponentiating the log loss. A confusing thing about this is that sometimes, normally perplexity is discussed in the context of generative models while is the log loss that is normally discussed in the context of discriminative models. However, the concepts of loss and perplexity still make sense in either context and can be used to compare models and predict their performance.

In conclusion, loss is a raw value calculated from the model and the observed data and perplexity is a normalization and interpretation of it. It is your interpretation of loss what defines what perplexity is exactly.
