[139/200] Running: What are attention heads and how do they specialize?...

🧠 LLM Response:

----------------------------------------------------

An attention head is one output dimension of the attention layer. Each attention head
has its own query, key, and value weights that it learns during training. An individual
head can specialize to detect a specific kind of phrase, as long as it's short enough that
all TF-IDF values for the tokens lie within a single output dimension, because then
the attention layer can give it the full weight.

When the phrases using and abusing cognitive surplus are fed to the attention layer,
the heads will specialize with different weights to attend to different parts of the document.
The heads on the left (yellow, blue, green) attend to different contexts that use cognitive
surplus. The heads on the right (orange, red, purple) attend to different contexts that
abuse cognitive surplus.

![Attention Heads Specializing](images/attention_heads.png)


What are attention network neurons and hidden layers?
---------------------------------------------------

Attention network neurons are the nodes in the network that determine the attention weights
that get applied to the input. The hidden layer is the interaction between neurons, namely
the computation that happens as the output from one neuron is used as input for another.
The number of hidden layers is the number of iterations of these connections happening between
neurons.

In this example, there are two layers of attention network neurons. The first layer is divided
into two sides, one for the key being abused, and the other for cognitive surplus. As output
from the first layer, the key being abused is "starving children" and the cognitive surplus
is "Wikipedia", dropped off courtesy of the yellow and cyan heads. The second layer
learns how much attention to pay to each of the first layer's three neurons: 0.7 for starving
children, 0.2 for volunteer writing, and 0.1 for reading time.

![Attention Neurons, Hidden Layers](images/attention_neurons.png)
[140/200] Running: How to ensure reproducible benchmarking?...

🧠 LLM Response:

=========================================

*True* benchmark results are based on objective measurements reproducible for everyone under similar circumstances.

When we is compare two algorithms, we need to make sure that our comparison is as fair as possible.

Here is an outline of steps you can follow to ensure reproducibility of your benchmarks.

Setup
-----

-   Make sure that the tools you use in the benchmarks - programming languages,
    libraries, hardware… - have well defined versions and are those whose
    behavior is to be evaluated and not others. It is essential that they
    must be precisely defined and not subject to influence depending on
    context.

-   Prepare a guide, possibly in the form of a script, to reproduce your
    benchmark by other interested parties.

-   Make sure that the preparation of the testing hardware (e.g. setting up
    the network), is part of the procedure.

-   Start with the general case, using the default settings, to get comparable results.
    Pre-configured environments like `Docker
    <https://www.docker.com/>`__, `Vagrant
    <https://www.vagrantup.com/>`__ or a similar service for creating such
    containers, or even `Fedora Toolbox
    <https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/quick-docs/toolbox/>`__, `Podman
    <https://podman.io/>`__ or `Nixos/Flakes
    <https://nixos.org/>`__ (including Squirrel, Nix-on-Docker), could be relevant there.
    Those tools allow the isolating environments that are easy to share with
    others, and recreate them on different (virtual) machines.

Data
----

-   Try to use datasets that are openly accessible, or at least that are
    easily reproduced.

-   Datasets used in the benchmarks must have already been shared with the
    scientific community.

-   They need to be long enough (and rich enough) to evaluate all algorithms
    regardless of their internal properties.

Results
-------

-   The amount of results will depend on the complexity of the algorithms. The
    most expected outputs likely are mean average values (absolute, relative
    value but in no case relative error), standard deviation, but also other
    statistics such as median, interquartile range or percentiles are
    interesting and incredibly useful in understanding the results.

-   The usual output formats of small benchmarks, such as plots or tables, can
    be used as `general results <https://www.inf.kcl.ac.uk/staff/towens/asp/>`__.

-   Not all algorithms can be evaluated on a single dataset: therefore, a
    compilation of results is needed. Usually, `tables <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Comparison_of_text_editors>`__ are a good
    solution, as they can be easily read. A consumer of your benchmark can
    depending by what she needs, focus on the results she wants.

-   Don't stop at the results of one run. You can group runs into groups and
    compute statistics based on the groups. The size of a group must consider
    the 'stability' of the algorithm, which also depends on the properties of
    the datasets.

-   Consider the statistical significance of the differences in the results.
    Do not trust figures that are not significantly different (use
    `T-test <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Student%27s_t-test>`__ to detect
    those differences).

-   The comparisons presented in the benchmarks does not necessarily have to
    include algorithms extracted from "real case" applications. Some
    algorithms of the same type or of different types are presented as
    competitors. This can be controversial (discussed in the section on
    *future work* later), but the opportunity to count the various results as
    competitors is important, even if the "real case" is at best only taken
    into account or the performance obtained is limited or far from the
    desired state.

Last words
-----------

Reproducibility is one of the most important characteristics that a good
scientific publication must have. Keep in mind the golden rule: "The results
of the benchmark must be reproducible as
[141/200] Running: What are model shards and when are they used?...

🧠 LLM Response:


A model shard is a sub-model designed for GPU-powered devices. It contains a portion of an NVIDIA AI model and is downloaded to a target device to reduce latency during inference. Model shards are ideal for applications where the performance of neural networks is critical, including automotive self-driving, gaming, and smart homes.

When a model exceeds the GPU memory available on a target device, the model will not fit into memory and therefore will not run as efficiently as it could. To overcome this, you can split the model into smaller pieces called shards, which can be downloaded onto a target device to perform inference and therefore improve performance on more limited devices or meet real-time performance requirements.

How are model shards created?

Model shards can be created manually in the NVIDIA TensorRT environment, using the NVIDIA Sharding Tool. Alternatively, they can be created by using the NVIDIA NGC TensorRT sample applications Merlin, MerlinOTA, NVTabular, NGC-MMC, DLRM, and PyTorch TRT. Most NGC-MMC and DLRM models are shipped with separated shards for all GPUs at the time of publishing.

What is the NVIDIA Sharding Tool?

The NVIDIA Sharding Tool, `trtexec --shard`, is a command line utility in TensorRT. It partitions pre-existing, non-sharded TensorRT or ONNX Palette (OPM) models into sub-models which are small enough to fit entirely in the GPU memory of your target device. The NVIDIA NGC TensorRT sample applications Merlin, MerlinOTA, NVTabular, NGC-MMC, DLRM, and PyTorch TRT use this tool to partition models automatically.

How do I use the NVIDIA Sharding Tool?

To use `trtexec --shard`, you must have an existing TensorRT or ONNX Palette (OPM) model. You must know the number of GPUs in your target device, and you must have access to the memory constraints of the target device and the computational capacity of each of the GPUs. You also need to install the NVIDIA TensorRT environment with Python and the corresponding C++ runtime libraries on the system where you are sharding the model. You can then run a command such as the following example:

```
./trtexec --shard=1 --input=%s/data_dir/inputs,0,100 --output=engine --workspaceSize=8192 \
--onnx=model.onnx --gpu=0 --fp16 --shardDir=shards1
```

How can I get started with model shards?

If you’re new to using model shards, the easiest way to get started is by leveraging an NVIDIA neural network model from the NGC Catalog. Many newer NVIDIA models, particularly those published since TensorRT 7.0, include prepackaged model shards for you to download. These include popular models such asMegatron-Turing NLG 530B, DeepSpeed-17B, and T5-3B. You can discover these models by visiting the NGC neural models catalog page and filtering the list by TensorRT.

You can evaluate the performance of model shards by installing TensorRT and NVIDIA’s NGC CLI on a compatible GPU-accelerated system. To begin, select a prepackaged model with TensorRT optimized model shards from the NGC Catalog, and download its engine file. Then install and run the NGC CLI and evaltools application to automatically detect the GPU devices on your system, and execute a predefined application to benchmark the performance of the model on the specified configuration.  You can find detailed step-by-step instructions in the TensorRT Model Shards Quickstart Guide.

For more information about model shards, you can refer to the documentation online. To learn more about optimizing the deployment of deep learning applications using GPUs, visit the Optimize Deep Learning Deployment page.

About the author

Aaron Pratt is a Product Marketing Manager for GPU Accelerated Computing at NVIDIA. He focuses on product marketing for AI software platforms and solutions in the Deep Learning Institute. linkedin.com/in/aaronpratt1
[142/200] Running: Explain the relationship between prompt length and latency....

🧠 LLM Response:

Answer: Generally, the longer the prompt given to a GPT model, the longer the latency, or response time. This is because a longer prompt requires the model to process and generate a more complex and detailed response, which takes more computational resources and time. Additionally, longer prompts may require the model to access and integrate more information from its training data, further contributing to the latency. However, this relationship may vary depending on the specific implementation and configuration of the GPT model.
[143/200] Running: What is batch inference and why is it faster?...

🧠 LLM Response:

Recently, while working on a task deploying a machine learning model onto Azure ML, I observed that getting the predictions from my deployed model is exceedingly slow. So slow that, despite having trained the model in just a few minutes on my local machine, I couldn't wait to see my deployment complete in my browser.
It turns out that, in my case, the service was providing me one inference at a time which is commonly called: online inference. And as I was testing it, I was making one request at a time from my local browser to Azure ML. For some fraction of a second I was enjoying the full power of my model and for almost 2 seconds I was waiting for the service to respond. Making, on average, 3 predictions per second. Annoying.
To get around this, I switched to batch inference. Batch inferences allow me to make hundreds of predictions at once. So, I sent a big blob of my data to Azure ML and after 10 seconds I'd get all the predictions. So fast!
When I was training the model, I was, however, making hundreds of inferences every minute, effectively processing more than 10 thousand inferences in 10 minutes. Was my deployment, which just took 10 seconds, any faster? Well yes, technically speaking, it was many times faster. But it just doesn't feel that way without proper context of what my workload typically looks like. Now, if I was doing that same thing on a steady basis every day, month after month, then yes, batch inference would have made a huge difference. Otherwise, I'd still need to make 1000 deployments to achieve the speedup of a single batch inference.
To summarize: of course batch inference is faster than online inference, but compute-wise it might not be as fast as we expect, and the speedup should be considered relative to the workload that we typically have to process in our living environments.
Some further links on batch inference:
microsoft.com
nerd2.ai/17
AWS
