    - Tegra Deep Neural Network (TDNN) Framework does this.
- Have to be cautious evicting things on GPU.
- CUDA has a cache as well! Not user visible.
- We have made 512 MiB bigger, so less of a problem.
- LU has the right curve for temporal locality.
- Each thread grabs a minibatch, need to make sure individual items don’t soak the cache too much and therefore make things inefficient.
- Deep cache does not have LRU. Need to use Fully Least Recent (FLR).
    - This throws out big chunks at a time to prevent the cache being fully consumed by smaller objects.
[151/200] Running: What is a prompt-truncation error?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 The prompt-truncation error happens when the VIC-20 is sending data to the printer after the character that initiates a linefeed command. During printer transfer, such as `PRINT#3,A$` and similar, this can result in the VIC-20 continuing to print data onto the same line. Obviously, this won't do in the VIC-20's motherboard schematic, so the internal error led beeps.

The flipside of the coin is shown below—when printing to a file, the VIC-20 will add the linefeed into the file:

```
LOAD "KEYWORD",6
POKE 37643,32: POKE 37644,255
```

Unpack and run the `KEYWORD.PRG` on the VIC-20, answer the questions regarding the serial printer, then exit with line number `1` and input the following:

```
  OPEN 3,8,3,"N0:USER.PRG"
  PRINT#3,A$
  CLOSE 3
  LOAD"USER",8
```

The `USER.PRG` will contain a linefeed character after the end of the `A$` string. You can get rid of it by storing the file contents into a string, like so:

```
OPEN 3,8,3,"N0:USER.PRG"
DIM A$(1): LINE INPUT#3,A$(1)
CLOSE 3
SAVE"USER",8
```

Last but not least, you can also fix such errors by not using a `PRINT#` statement while transferring data from memory to the printer. Instead, use the `BPUT#` statement with binary mode (preceded by a comma), which will always print the next record on a new line:

```
OPEN 3,8,3,"N0:USER.PRG"
BSAVE "C0:OSBASIC.BIN", &H800, &H7FF
CLOSE 3
```

```
OPEN 3,8,3,",O"
BLOAD "C0:OSBASIC.BIN",&H800
CLOSE 3
```

```
OPEN 3,8,3,"N0:USER.PRG"
BLOAD "C0:OSBASIC.BIN",&H800
CLOSE 3
```

Then, of course, you can still load the contents into memory with `LOAD`. Since the data is due to the `BLOAD` command binary, any `LOAD` statements will ignore any unwanted character(s) at the end of the file.

Other platform than VIC-20? Sure! You can also see some of these nice features on other 6502 machines, such as 64ers and 6510s, such as Commodore 64, Commodore 128, and their expansions. Eventually, it is also available on the Apple 2 and Atari platforms. In general, any 6502 machine that can display text file formats with linefeed characters would do such a trick. In other words, on any 6502 platform, you can print binary data to a file, run the results on actual hardware or in an emulator, compare to the original data and there you have a conclusively quick and dirty data dirty data comparison without programmatic errors.

Below is an example of two compressed data files that use different checksum routines to ensure data integrity. Both check routine(s)' own end-of-data, but sometimes that's not good enough.

Let's see how it looks like with the similar configurations on an Atari 800 XL:

Here is a capture of the VICmini monitor determining the VIC-20's open firmware file's data integrity:

If you know any localization to any other platforms (besides the Atari, Apple 2, and the 6502 family in general), hit me with an email or comment, or submit a PR or fork on GitHub!
[152/200] Running: Explain the role of tokenizer config files....

🧠 LLM Response:


Tokenizer config files specify how text data should be converted to tokens for training a model or generating text.

The most important setting is the tokenizer type. For example, there are config files for subword tokenization, byte-level BPE, and sentencepiece unigram LM.

Apart from the tokenizer type, there are many other optional fields, such as:
- vocab\_size: specifies the size of the vocabulary (number of tokens)
- min\_len: specifies the minimum length for a token to be kept
- special\_tokens: specifies a list of special tokens, like <s>, </s>, <unk>
- unk\_token: specifies the out-of-vocabulary token that is used to replace unknown words

Tokenizer config files can be customized and act as a bridge between raw text and a vocabulary that the model uses for training and generating text. Without proper tokenizer configuration files, the model might not perform as expected.
