[155/200] Running: What is inference latency and why is it important?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Inference latency refers to the time it takes for a machine learning model to process and make predictions on input data. In other words, it is the delay between submitting a request to a model and receiving the predicted output.

Inference latency is an important metric because it can affect the responsiveness and usability of a machine learning application. For example, in a real-time object detection system, a high inference latency could mean that objects are not detected and displayed on the screen until several seconds after they have passed, making the system less useful.

Inference latency is also an important consideration in the design and deployment of machine learning models. For example, certain model architectures or hardware platforms may be better suited for low-latency inference, while others may be more appropriate for batch processing or offline analysis.

There are a variety of techniques and best practices that can be used to optimize inference latency, including model pruning, quantization, and optimization for specific hardware platforms. It is important to carefully consider the requirements and constraints of a given application in order to choose the most appropriate approach for reducing inference latency.
