[185/200] Running: How to enable/disable kv caching in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:

=================================================

Cache information can be stored for various checks that are only known at runtime,
meaning that it would be infeasible to perform early checks without storing any information.
For example, when creating a :class:`~charm4py.periodic.Monitor`, it is unclear for the
library how many periods should be requested. Thus, the user can explicitly request a number
through a key-value (kv) entry first time they request such information.
This information is then cached to prevent requesting the same information again.

Without such caching, requesting, for example, a :class:`~charm4py.periodic.Monitor`
multiple times would always create the external control program (ECP) or load
the address manager. Hence, it is generally useful to enable such caching by
setting the following environment variables::

    export CHARM_KV_TEST_DEFAULT_SIZE=1000000

The exact, **experimental** approach for setting these values is dependent on your kernel::

    export CHARM_TEST_ENV=CHARM_KV_TEST_DEFAULT_SIZE=1000000

To prevent caching all together, you can set the ``CHARM_KV_CACHE_DISABLE`` variable::

    export CHARM_KV_CACHE_DISABLE=1

With this, no caching is performed at runtime.
[186/200] Running: What are challenges of multilingual streaming inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:

---------------------------------------------------------

These are some of the main challenges that large language model-based multilingual streaming inference can face:

* Supporting many low-resource languages
	+ There is a long tail of languages which are used rarely and typically have insufficient and imbalanced data sets.
* Disparities in the quality of language model training
	+ The quality of the language model training is dependent on the availability and diversity of the training data.
	+ Data sets used for training language models typically have corpus features that are directly reflected in system performance.
	+ For example, languages that have more training data tend to have better results on downstream tasks, while languages that have fewer training data tend to perform worse.
	+ Some languages have larger training datasets than others, which can lead to imbalances in model performance across languages.
	+ These imbalances can lead to gap of model performance between major languages and minor languages.
* Handling low-quality integrations
	+ Multilingual language models need to integrated with application for uses in real life that are practical and effective.
	+ Some integrations have low quality and do not give accurate and smooth results.
	+ These integrations often require customization from both human users and machine learning engineers.
	+ High quality integrations need support from application-sided improvements and streamlined machine learning engineering.
* Providing real-time interaction and adaptation
	+ People interacet in real-time.
	+ Systems that are not able to handle the real-time interaction can suffer from delays and poor user experience.
	+ Current systems that provide this functionality have to use small models and/or apportion capacities.
	+ Systems that use apportionment have to deal with complex resource allocation systems.
	+ Systems that use small models suffer from worse performance than larger models.
* Ensuring data privacy and security
	+ Systems need to ensure there are proper protections and encryption to prevent unauthorized access, data breaches, and leaking Protected Health Information (PHI).

Some best practices and methods for handling some of these challanges are listed in the [Documentation page](streaming_ml_inference_multilingual.md#best-practices).
